R"VG(
# futuristic9.vg (H-04's second pass): the medical log (a clinic's day: who came, with what, what was done; births and deaths; the
# people's own ills and remedies) and the memorial (`@event ... memorial`: the dead of a disaster, a war, a fire, kept by name).

# ---- the medical log ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

genre.medical (words=24..120)
  {md.head}\n{log.t1} {md.line}\n{log.t2} {md.line}\n{log.t3} {md.line}\n{log.t5} {md.line}
  {md.head}\n{log.t1} {md.line}\n{log.t3} {md.line}\n{log.t4} {md.line}
  {md.head}\n{log.t2} {md.line}\n{log.t3} {md.line}\n{log.t4} {md.line}\n{log.t5} {md.line}

md.head (formula people=3)
  Medical log, {$home} clinic, cycle {#year:digits}.
  The clinic at {$home}: the day's cases.
  Clinic, {$home}, {season.now}, cycle {#year:digits}.
  (has:$home.quarter) The clinic of {$home.quarter}, {$home}: the day.
  Day book of the clinic at {$home}. Cycle {#year:digits}.

md.line
  (4 once) {md.who}, {md.ill}. {md.treat}
  (3 once) {md.who}, {md.ill}. {md.treat}
  (1 once) {md.who}, {md.well}. {md.talk}
  (1 once !has:.nobirth) {md.birth} {=.birth}
  (1 once) {md.death}
  (1 once) {md.note}
  (1 once sick=1) Further cases of the sickness. Isolation ward full.
  (1 once stage>=1 !has:.birth) No births this cycle. {=.nobirth}
  (1 once stage>=2) The last nurse at the clinic has gone. The machines are tending the sick.

md.who (people=12)
  A patient of [thirty-four|forty|fifty-one|sixty-six|seventy|eighty-two]
  A child of [two|four|six|seven|nine|eleven]
  An old man from the {fut.block}
  A woman from {city2}
  A worker from the {fut.green}
  A girl from the school
  Someone from the {craft} workshop
  A keeper of the {herd}
  The watchman at the {fut.gate}
  (has:$home.quarter) A woman from {$home.quarter}
  A boy of {n} from the {fut.block}
  A singer, the week before {festival}
  One of the players of {instrument}
  A man of a hundred and four, who is proud of it
  Twins of {n}, one ill and one sympathetic
  A runner from the games
  A woman expecting her {nth} child
  A newcomer from {city2}, homesick and polite

md.ill (share=0.7)
  fever
  a broken wrist from a fall
  a cough that will not go
  a burn from the light towers
  a cut hand from the {fut.green}
  pains in the chest
  a bite from a {beast}
  headaches since {festival}
  too much {drink} at {festival}
  a splinter of {material} in the eye
  (seasons=4) the {season.now} cough
  sleeplessness, three weeks of it
  a rash from the {crop2}
  stung by something in the {fut.green}
  earache from {instrument}
  swallowed a piece from {fut.game}
  a twisted ankle on the long stair
  light-blindness from the observation deck
  sick from {weather.now}
  an old wound aching in {weather.now}
  kicked by one of the {herd}
  a nosebleed that would not stop
  a burn from {fuel}
  a fish bone, stuck
  dizziness in the lifts
  a fall from a ladder in the {fut.green}

md.treat (share=0.8)
  Treated. Discharged.
  Kept overnight.
  Sent home with instructions about {food} that they will not follow.
  Referred to {city}.
  Better by evening.
  Treated the old way, with {drink} and patience, at the family's request.
  Treated; the {~remedy} given as well, because the grandmother insisted.
  Set, splinted and lectured.
  Nothing to be done but wait. Waiting.
  Treated, and told to stay away from the {herd} for a week.
  Cleaned, closed, and given a {colour.holy} ribbon for bravery.
  Treated. Will be back; they always are.
  Machines did the work; I held the hand and talked about {fut.game}.
  Treated, and given {drink} with honey, which is not medicine and works anyway.
  Bound in {colour.holy} cloth, as the old ones bound wounds, at the patient's request.
  Treated. The patient swore {oath} through all of it.

md.well
  tired, wants to talk
  nothing wrong, and worried
  lonely; says it is the knee
  well, and came to say so
  wants a second opinion on a first opinion nobody gave
md.talk (share=0.85)
  Talked to for an hour. Better.
  Sent home with {drink}.
  Told to come back if it gets worse, and to keep away from the {herd} meanwhile.
  Given a chair, {drink} and the news, and left happier.
  Walked back to the {fut.block} with them; it was on my way, more or less.

md.birth
  Birth, healthy. Mother well.
  A girl, {fut.when.past}. To be named on the third day, as is the custom.
  Twins. The family asked for {colour.holy} cloths for them; done.
  A boy, small and loud. The grandmother has already sworn {oath} over him.
  A birth in the {fut.green}, which was not planned. Mother and child well; the {crop2} less so.
  A daughter, after a long night. The father fainted, which the mother will never let him forget.
md.death (share=0.7)
  A death, expected: the oldest hand in the {craft} workshop, in the night. The family have sent for {colour.mourn} cloth.
  A death: a patient of {nbig} years, peacefully, with the {festival} songs playing.
  A death we did not expect, in the {fut.block}. The family have asked for {colour.mourn} and silence.
  A death, after a long illness; the family sang the {festival} songs at the end, with {instrument}, all of them.
  An old keeper of the {herd} died this morning. The {herd} were brought to the window. It was what was asked.
md.note (share=0.7)
  Supplies of the {~remedy} low again.
  {#n} injuries from {pastime}. All of them deny it.
  {weather.now} brought in {#n|case}.
  Clinic closed {fut.when.past} for cleaning, to complaints.
  {#n|child} in for checks before {festival}. All well, one bored.
  The {herd} got into the clinic garden. No injuries, except to the garden.

# ---- the memorial -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

genre.memorial (words=10..70)
  {mem.head}\n{@event sickness,famine,flood,quake,battle,sack,siege,impact,flare,eruption,winter,exodus,death,fire,storm,accident,drought,abandoned,unrest,war memorial 80}\n{mem.end}

mem.head (formula people=3)
  In memory.
  This memorial was raised at {$home} by those who remained.
  Read this aloud, once a year, on this day.
  For the archive, and for the wall of the {fut.common}.
  Kept at {holyplace}. Touch the names; they are cut deep enough.

memorial.sickness
  Of those who died of {$e.sickness} at {$e.place}, {#$e.value:about} of them. We say their names here, every year, on this day.
  Of those {$e.sickness} took at {$e.place}, in the {#$e.year:ord} year. The names follow.
  Of those {$e.sickness} took, and of the ones who nursed them, with {drink} and with their hands.
memorial.famine
  Of those who died in the hunger at {$e.place}. There was food in the world, and not where they were.
  Of those who starved, in the {#$e.year:ord} year, while the {crop} failed.
memorial.flood
  Of those the water took at {$e.place}.
  Of those drowned at {$e.place} when the water came over the marks, {#$e.value:about} of them.
  Of those {river} took at {$e.place}, with the {herd} and the {crop} and the houses.
memorial.quake
  Of those who died when the ground moved under {$e.place}.
memorial.battle
  Of those who fell at {$e.place}, on both sides, {#$e.value:about} of them.
  Of those who fought at {$e.place} in {$e.war}, and did not come home to the {herd}.
  Of the dead of {$e.place}, ours and theirs, under the same {colour.mourn}.
memorial.sack
  Of the people of {$e.place}, who were not given the chance to leave.
memorial.siege
  Of those who held {$e.place} in {$e.war}, and those who starved there.
memorial.war
  Of everyone {$e.war} took, whatever side they stood on.
memorial.impact
  Of those who were under the stone when it fell.
memorial.flare
  Of those who were out under the sun when it burned.
memorial.eruption
  Of those {mountain} buried.
memorial.winter
  Of those the long winter took.
memorial.drought
  Of those the dry years took, a few at a time, so that nobody counted until the end.
memorial.exodus
  Of those who left {$e.place} and were not heard of again, {#$e.value:about} of them.
memorial.abandoned
  Of {$e.place}, and everyone who lived there, when it was still lived in.
memorial.unrest
  Of those who died in the streets of {$e.place}, for reasons that were good enough then.
memorial.fire
  Of those the fire took at {$e.place}, and of the houses, {#$e.value:about} of them.
  Of {$e.place}, which burned, and of those who carried water from {watersrc} all night.
memorial.storm
  Of those the storm took at {$e.place}.
memorial.accident
  Of those who died at {$e.place}, {#$e.value:about} of them, in the disaster nobody would call an accident.
  Of those who were working at {$e.place} that day, and those who went in after them.
  Of the dead of {$e.place}, {#$e.value:about} of them, whose names are cut here in {colour.holy}.
memorial.death
  Of {$e.a:full}, who was ours.
  Of {$e.a:full}. {death.went}, they say; we say: missed.
  Of {$e.a:full}, for whom the {colour.mourn} cloths were hung at {holyplace}.
  Of {$e.a:full}, who kept {craft} alive, and the {herd}, and us.
memorial.none
  Of all who lived here, in every age, whatever they called themselves.
  Of everyone the records forgot to name, and everyone they named wrongly.
  Of the {people} of {$home}, the ones before us and the ones before them.

mem.end (formula)
  We remember.
  The names are kept in the archive. Ask for them.
  Say one of the names before you go.
  {blessing}.
)VG"
